## 2. Components: catalogued only

- **Use only catalogued components.** `CATALOG.md` (next to the components) lists every component with its
  props, variants and when to use it. Read it before building anything. [review]
- **A new component enters the catalog and the gallery in the same change.** The gallery is a dev-only page
  (a route such as `/dev/gallery`; pick one and name it here) that renders every component in every variant and state, in the widest locale, at
  375 px and 1440 px. A component missing from either is a review blocker. [review] [not yet: a guard test that every exported component appears in CATALOG.md and the gallery]
- **Typed options, not copies.** A component that nearly fits gets a typed `variant`, `tone` or `size` option.
  Never copy it, never restyle it from a page. A page's `className` (or style hook) only places a component:
  margin, width, grid or flex placement. [review] [ci: dupes] (a stack with no `dupes` gate: [not yet: copy-paste gate])
- Every exported component exports its props type (`<Name>Props`). [review]
- Two blocks of the same markup become one component; the duplicate-code gate only shrinks. [ci: dupes] (a stack with no `dupes` gate: [not yet: copy-paste gate])

## 3. Tokens only

- Colours, spacing, radii, shadows, type sizes, line heights, z-indices and motion durations come from the
  design tokens. No hex colours, no magic pixel values, no inline styles in components or pages. [ci: tokens]
- **Colour means status only.** Accent colour marks the one primary action and status (success, warning,
  danger, pending, info). Decoration is neutral. Status is never colour alone: add an icon or text. [review] [ux]
- Contrast is AA (4.5:1 body text, 3:1 large text and UI parts), **measured** on the rendered page (the
  computed colours, both themes), not judged by eye. [ux] [not yet: an automated contrast check in `wf check`]

## 4. Layout and responsiveness

- **Mobile first.** Design and build the 375 px layout first, then add columns. Check every surface at
  the `dev.viewports` widths (**375 and 1440 px** by default; add a width where the layout has a breakpoint
  between them). Zero horizontal page scroll at every width. [ux]
- **Verification is by LOOKING** at screenshots in the widest locale (`{{WIDEST_LOCALE}}`). A zero-overflow
  measurement (`scrollWidth === clientWidth`) is not verification: it passes while a word shreds
  mid-syllable, a sentence clips inside a scrolling cell or a button sits off-screen. Open the PNGs. [ux]
- The visual check aborts when the page is not in the widest locale; a narrower locale passes optimistically. [ux]
- **Container queries for rows in columns.** A row that lives inside a card, a sidebar or a column adapts to
  its container's width, not the viewport's. [review]
- **Tap targets at least 44 x 44 px** with 8 px between neighbours; nothing important only on hover. [ux]
- Images have `alt` (a key, or empty for decoration) and explicit `width`/`height` (or aspect ratio), so the
  layout does not shift. [review] [ux]
- Reserve space for loading content (skeletons of the final shape), no layout shift when data arrives. [ux]

## 5. States are designed, not defaulted

Every view designs all of these before it is done: [review] [ux]
- **Empty**: says what this is, why it is empty and the one action that fills it.
- **Loading**: skeleton in the final shape; a slow action shows progress, not a frozen button.
- **Error**: says what failed in plain words, what the user can do, and keeps their work.
- **Never connected / never set up**: a third state, distinct from "nothing yet" and "set up and quiet".
  A device that has never called home is not "online, no events".
- **Pending shows pending.** A submitted-but-unconfirmed change (queued command, unconfirmed payment,
  awaiting approval) looks pending until confirmed; it never shows success early.
- **Partial permission**: what a user cannot do is either hidden (if they never can) or disabled with the
  reason (if they could under other conditions).

## 6. Actions and feedback

- **One primary action per view**, visually dominant; everything else is secondary or tertiary. [ux]
- **Disabled controls say why.** Use `aria-disabled="true"` (not the `disabled` attribute, which blocks
  focus and tooltips) and a reason the user can reach: a tooltip on hover AND focus, and on touch a tap
  that shows the reason. [review] [ux]
- **A refused write keeps the input and says why**, next to the field it concerns, using the server's
  reason (a message key). The user never retypes a form. [review] [ux]
- **Destructive actions** are visually distinct, confirmed, and name the object ("Delete invoice 1042").
  **The destructive verb is lexically distinct from the dismiss verb in every locale**: if one word means
  both "cancel" and "revoke" in a locale, the confirm dialog must use a different word for one of them. [review] [i18n review]
- Undo beats confirm for reversible actions. [review]
- **No stacked descriptions**: one title and at most one line of help per section; a paragraph above a form
  that repeats its labels is noise. [ux]
- **Truncation shows a tooltip only when the text is actually clipped** (measure, then attach). A tooltip on
  text that is fully visible is noise; a clipped value without one hides data. [review]
- Errors say how to fix them; success says what happened and what is next. [ux]

## 7. Copy and language

- Plain, concrete words in the user's language and domain; no filler ("Seamlessly manage your..."). [ux]
- Every visible string, `aria-label`, `title`, `alt`, `placeholder` and `sr-only` text is an i18n key. Library
  defaults that ship English (`<span class="sr-only">Close</span>`) are replaced when the component is
  added. [not yet: i18n check] [review]
- Numbers, dates, currencies and lists are formatted with the locale's `Intl` formatter. [review]
<!-- tune: house copy and formatting conventions. The lines below are examples from earlier projects; keep, change or delete each. -->
- Example: pin `hourCycle` (for example `h23`) where a locale's default clock surprises users. [review]
- Example: no dashes as sentence punctuation in UI copy; use full stops and commas. [review]
<!-- /tune -->

## 8. Accessibility

- Every input has a visible label; errors are linked with `aria-describedby`; focus is visible on every
  interactive element; the tab order follows the visual order. [review] [ux]
- Dialogs trap focus and return it on close; `Escape` closes non-destructive dialogs. [review]
- Motion respects `prefers-reduced-motion`. [review]

## 9. Anti-template rules (the ux-critic's slop list)

A screen that looks generated loses trust. Each of these is a finding: [ux]
- gradient-blob or mesh-gradient heroes; glassmorphism used as decoration;
- three identical feature cards with an icon, a title and two lines;
- emoji used as icons; stock "rocket", "sparkles" or "magic wand" imagery for ordinary features;
- everything rounded with a soft shadow; cards inside cards inside cards;
- centred everything, oversized hero type on an app screen, marketing copy inside the product;
- filler copy ("Unlock the power of...", "Seamlessly"), lorem ipsum, fake testimonials or metrics;
- a dashboard of big-number tiles that answer no question the user has;
- a different look for the same job on two screens.
Distinctive means: the layout follows the job, density fits the user (an operator console is dense, a
first-run flow is calm), type and spacing come from the scale, and one accent marks the next step.

